  "slug": "time-controlled-criticality-bc5c",
  "summary": "Several preprints extend tensor-network, semiclassical and GPU trajectory methods to ~100-qubit devices, QEC codes and DMRG-based state preparation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is scaling classical computational tools that either emulate quantum hardware or compile resources for it. On the simulation side, pre-trajectory sampling with batched GPU execution now handles mid-circuit measurements and general Kraus channels; compiled Clifford-augmented MPS reach distance-9 surface codes under coherent noise; a polynomial-cost effective-Hamiltonian method models couplings in 96-transmon processors and predicts 100-qubit quench dynamics; and cluster spin-LSC treats a sensor plus strongly coupled bath spins quantum mechanically to simulate spin-qubit decoherence. On state preparation, two works load DMRG matrix product states onto hardware by exploiting spin symmetry—abelian block-sparse protocols (Toffoli counts cut up to 135x) and non-Abelian SU(2) via Wigner–Eckart reduction—while PP-VarQITE combines Pauli propagation with variational imaginary time for shallow thermal-state circuits up to 98 qubits. A separate result reports an opto-electronic Ising machine with single-shot readout of over 100,000 spins.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
